WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:03.000
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy-nominated actor,

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:04.334
writer, and musician.

00:00:04.334 --> 00:00:06.267
He's currently starring
in "What Happens Later,"

00:00:06.267 --> 00:00:07.801
which is in theaters
November 3rd.

00:00:07.801 --> 00:00:08.801
Let's take a look.

00:00:08.801 --> 00:00:12.501
-Still a music snob.
Oh, geez.

00:00:12.501 --> 00:00:14.534
Remember,
you used to hide my CDs

00:00:14.534 --> 00:00:15.968
when our friends came over?

00:00:15.968 --> 00:00:18.968
Hey, when our friends came over,
you used to hide my CDs.

00:00:18.968 --> 00:00:21.033
-One CD, one time,

00:00:21.033 --> 00:00:22.567
the "Partridge Family
Christmas" album.

00:00:22.567 --> 00:00:24.667
And I did that
to spare you the humiliation.

00:00:24.667 --> 00:00:26.334
-So my taste of music
was humiliating.

00:00:26.334 --> 00:00:27.934
-Oh, yeah.
-Oh, yeah? To who?

00:00:27.934 --> 00:00:29.434
-To you.
-No, not to me.

00:00:29.434 --> 00:00:31.133
To you.
-To me.

00:00:31.133 --> 00:00:33.834
And to you.
-Sheesh.

00:00:33.834 --> 00:00:35.067
-Please welcome back
to the show,

00:00:35.067 --> 00:00:37.267
our friend David Duchovny,
everybody.

00:00:37.267 --> 00:00:40.267
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:40.267 --> 00:00:45.968
♪♪

00:00:45.968 --> 00:00:51.634
♪♪

00:00:51.634 --> 00:00:53.801
-Take a seat.
-Thank you.

00:00:53.801 --> 00:00:55.267
-It's lovely to have you here.

00:00:55.267 --> 00:00:56.601
-It's nice to be back, Seth.

00:00:56.601 --> 00:00:59.501
-You get to be in
a romantic comedy with Meg Ryan.

00:00:59.501 --> 00:01:01.334
-I know.
-She directed it as well.

00:01:01.334 --> 00:01:02.968
-I know. I know these things.
-Shot it.

00:01:02.968 --> 00:01:04.434
You shot it?
Yeah. These are not --

00:01:04.434 --> 00:01:06.267
I'm not telling you
anything you don't know.

00:01:06.267 --> 00:01:09.701
-Really I did?
Whew.

00:01:09.701 --> 00:01:11.801
-Good time to be
David Duchovny.

00:01:11.801 --> 00:01:13.667
You shot most of it
in an airport.

00:01:13.667 --> 00:01:15.100
-Yeah. Now it gets worse...
-Wow.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:16.133
-As you go on.
-Meg --

00:01:16.133 --> 00:01:17.501
Meg did not do herself
any favors

00:01:17.501 --> 00:01:19.734
because it's not easy
to shoot a movie in an airport.

00:01:19.734 --> 00:01:22.167
-No, because, well,
it was a regional airport,

00:01:22.167 --> 00:01:24.634
so it did shut down
at like 9:00,

00:01:24.634 --> 00:01:26.868
which is when we did
most of our shooting.

00:01:26.868 --> 00:01:29.400
But we also had to shoot
sometimes during the day,

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:31.434
and there are just
people going

00:01:31.434 --> 00:01:33.033
to and from
wherever they're going.

00:01:33.033 --> 00:01:34.367
-So it's a working airport.

00:01:34.367 --> 00:01:35.567
-It's a working airport.
-It's not an abandoned airport.

00:01:35.567 --> 00:01:37.100
-And I don't know
if you know this,

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:39.634
but it's illegal to tell anybody
to do anything in an airport.

00:01:39.634 --> 00:01:42.367
I've never tried to do it
before, but like, you can't say,

00:01:42.367 --> 00:01:44.534
Seth, can you just sit there
for a moment

00:01:44.534 --> 00:01:46.167
while David and Meg
shoot this scene

00:01:46.167 --> 00:01:48.200
and you're like, no,
I've got to get to my gate.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:49.567
-Interesting.
-Can't do it.

00:01:49.567 --> 00:01:51.734
-So there's no --
There's no crowd control.

00:01:51.734 --> 00:01:52.901
-Zero.

00:01:52.901 --> 00:01:54.334
-And if I want to come over
and be like,

00:01:54.334 --> 00:01:56.367
I'm a big fan of both of you
while you're filming.

00:01:56.367 --> 00:01:58.467
-That's familiar
because that happened.

00:01:58.467 --> 00:01:59.734
[ Laughter ]

00:01:59.734 --> 00:02:02.367
Yeah, we would do these --
It's -- there's like long

00:02:02.367 --> 00:02:04.734
walk-and talk scenes
in this movie like eight pages,

00:02:04.734 --> 00:02:07.267
and we'd just be strolling
through the airport

00:02:07.267 --> 00:02:08.767
and, you know,
people would recognize Meg,

00:02:08.767 --> 00:02:09.968
or they'd recognize me.

00:02:09.968 --> 00:02:12.133
And I remember we came
after like seven pages

00:02:12.133 --> 00:02:15.300
and we were just like, high wire
acting it like we got it.

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:16.868
We did seven pages.
We're there.

00:02:16.868 --> 00:02:18.767
We're finally at the stop point

00:02:18.767 --> 00:02:20.367
and this guy goes,
"Hey, it's Meg Ryan."

00:02:20.367 --> 00:02:21.901
And I was, like,
I was in the middle of my line.

00:02:21.901 --> 00:02:23.801
I just said,
"That's not helpful."

00:02:23.801 --> 00:02:25.334
[ Laughter ]

00:02:25.334 --> 00:02:26.367
And then I was back here,

00:02:26.367 --> 00:02:27.634
and Meg was like,
"That was amazing."

00:02:27.634 --> 00:02:29.634
You just like...
-[ Laughing ]

00:02:29.634 --> 00:02:30.968
-I thought it was pretty polite,
"That's not helpful."

00:02:30.968 --> 00:02:32.434
-I mean, I do,
it's very polite.

00:02:32.434 --> 00:02:35.033
I also want to give a break
to a traveler

00:02:35.033 --> 00:02:36.801
at a regional airport
in Arkansas.

00:02:36.801 --> 00:02:39.200
I don't blame them for being
a little thrown by the fact

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:40.868
that Duchovny and Meg Ryan
are coming down the way.

00:02:40.868 --> 00:02:42.834
-No, I -- I --
It just wasn't helpful.

00:02:42.834 --> 00:02:44.234
That's all I'm saying.
-Yeah, you're right.

00:02:44.234 --> 00:02:46.100
You know what? I think it was --
-We're doing a scene.

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:47.100
-You gave him
a very honest fair...

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:48.334
-I wasn't saying
"You're an [bleep]."

00:02:48.334 --> 00:02:49.367
I'm just saying
you're not being helpful.

00:02:49.367 --> 00:02:50.934
[ Laughter ]

00:02:50.934 --> 00:02:53.133
-Where do you, like -- What
is a dressing room situation

00:02:53.133 --> 00:02:55.167
when you're shooting
in an airport?

00:02:55.167 --> 00:02:57.634
-I was in a yoga room.

00:02:57.634 --> 00:02:58.868
This was a fancy regional...

00:02:58.868 --> 00:03:01.000
-The Fayetteville airport
has a yoga room?

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:02.701
-Had a yoga room.

00:03:02.701 --> 00:03:05.133
-Well, now did people were is
the idea that they'll do yoga?

00:03:05.133 --> 00:03:06.968
-I call it a yoga room.
It was a room.

00:03:06.968 --> 00:03:07.968
-Yeah.
-It said --

00:03:07.968 --> 00:03:09.133
[ Laughter ]

00:03:09.133 --> 00:03:10.667
There was nothing else
in there that said yoga.

00:03:10.667 --> 00:03:12.100
-Okay.
-[ Chuckles ]

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:13.801
But it was called Yoga room.
-Oh, wow.

00:03:13.801 --> 00:03:14.934
-It was my room.

00:03:14.934 --> 00:03:16.534
Nobody came in to do yoga
while I was there.

00:03:16.534 --> 00:03:17.567
-Okay.

00:03:17.567 --> 00:03:19.000
And I didn't do
any yoga in there,

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:20.634
but it was --
it was sweet, it was.

00:03:20.634 --> 00:03:22.567
There was no gong.
There was no foam roller.

00:03:22.567 --> 00:03:24.334
-But.
-Yeah, otherwise you just --

00:03:24.334 --> 00:03:26.901
There was just
an air of yoga about it.

00:03:26.901 --> 00:03:28.367
-It smelled more of Cinnabon.

00:03:28.367 --> 00:03:30.133
-Okay. Yeah.
-To be honest with you.

00:03:30.133 --> 00:03:31.934
-I mean, I think
almost every room in an airport

00:03:31.934 --> 00:03:33.734
is kind of a Cinnabon room,
if there's any.

00:03:33.734 --> 00:03:35.734
-I prefer that
to rank human sweat.

00:03:35.734 --> 00:03:37.300
-Yeah. That's true.

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:38.434
Yeah. You know what?
-Anytime.

00:03:38.434 --> 00:03:39.701
-Sold.

00:03:39.701 --> 00:03:41.234
I want to ask you a question.
I know you grew up

00:03:41.234 --> 00:03:43.234
in New York City,
Lower East Side, right?

00:03:43.234 --> 00:03:45.300
-Yeah.
-And -- And you now...

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:47.434
-Yeah. Sioux Falls.

00:03:47.434 --> 00:03:48.467
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:48.467 --> 00:03:50.267
-Sioux Falls?
[ Chuckles ]

00:03:50.267 --> 00:03:52.167
-I grew up in Sioux Falls.

00:03:52.167 --> 00:03:53.501
[ Laughter ]

00:03:53.501 --> 00:03:55.267
-How?
We're about --

00:03:55.267 --> 00:03:56.667
You know it's
trick-or-treating tomorrow.

00:03:56.667 --> 00:03:58.734
What were your -- What was trick
or treating like growing up

00:03:58.734 --> 00:04:00.901
in New York City?

00:04:00.901 --> 00:04:02.834
-Uh, fraught.
-Yeah.

00:04:02.834 --> 00:04:05.734
Was it as bad as I
might think it was in that era?

00:04:05.734 --> 00:04:07.501
-Yeah.
I mean, well, you know,

00:04:07.501 --> 00:04:08.968
you wanted to find
a big building

00:04:08.968 --> 00:04:10.467
because then you could
just get in and get out.

00:04:10.467 --> 00:04:13.601
-Yeah.
-You could do 100 apartments.

00:04:13.601 --> 00:04:17.567
And you'd have your your loot,
and you'd go home for the year.

00:04:17.567 --> 00:04:21.200
But the older kids also knew
that that was going on,

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:23.801
and they would lay in wait
for the younger kids.

00:04:23.801 --> 00:04:25.167
They were --
Yeah, aww.

00:04:25.167 --> 00:04:26.601
[ Laughter ]

00:04:26.601 --> 00:04:27.934
They were super lazy.

00:04:27.934 --> 00:04:30.067
And they would just let us go
and get all the candy

00:04:30.067 --> 00:04:31.901
and then like, we'll take that.
-So they just --

00:04:31.901 --> 00:04:34.167
You got candy mugged
as a kid in New York City?

00:04:34.167 --> 00:04:35.167
-Sure did.
-Wow.

00:04:35.167 --> 00:04:36.300
-Yeah.

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:38.534
And one time, you know,
trick-or-treat,

00:04:38.534 --> 00:04:40.901
you know, like, the --
it's trick-or-treat,

00:04:40.901 --> 00:04:42.567
but you never think
of the treat, right?

00:04:42.567 --> 00:04:43.734
Or you never think
of the trick.

00:04:43.734 --> 00:04:45.501
-Yeah, you assume
the treat is implied.

00:04:45.501 --> 00:04:48.100
You forget that you've also
asked for a trick, right?

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:50.100
-So I remember knocking
on this,

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:52.367
this door and this old lady
must have been,

00:04:52.367 --> 00:04:54.434
like, 80 years old,
opened it.

00:04:54.434 --> 00:04:56.067
I say she's 80,
but I was like 8,

00:04:56.067 --> 00:04:57.501
so she might have been 35.

00:04:57.501 --> 00:04:59.901
[ Laughter ]

00:04:59.901 --> 00:05:05.000
And -- And then I said,
"Trick-or-treat"

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:06.167
And she was like,
"Trick."

00:05:06.167 --> 00:05:10.701
And she just turned
and did a somersault.

00:05:10.701 --> 00:05:12.434
And then she was like, "You."

00:05:12.434 --> 00:05:14.033
I was like,
"I'll take my candy."

00:05:14.033 --> 00:05:16.100
It's like, "No, you got to do --
You got to do the trick."

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:19.200
So then I did a somersault
and she gave me my candy,

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:20.934
and then I got mugged
for the candy.

00:05:20.934 --> 00:05:21.934
[ Laughter ]

00:05:21.934 --> 00:05:23.067
-You said to those bullies,

00:05:23.067 --> 00:05:24.067
you're like,
"You owe me a somersault."

00:05:24.067 --> 00:05:25.400
-Yeah, let's see
some somersaults.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:28.400
-Do you -- have you --
Are you in your apartment now,

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:31.534
or do people come by your door
like, are you one of the...?

00:05:31.534 --> 00:05:33.400
-Well, they did
when I lived in Newark.

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:34.501
I don't live in New York
anymore.

00:05:34.501 --> 00:05:35.501
-Gotcha.
-Yeah.

00:05:35.501 --> 00:05:38.133
In LA, no, nobody comes by.
-Nobody.

00:05:38.133 --> 00:05:40.067
-So it's just a regular day.
-A drive by, drive by,

00:05:40.067 --> 00:05:42.167
8-year-old behind the wheel,
"Trick-or-treat."

00:05:42.167 --> 00:05:43.167
[ Laughter ]

00:05:43.167 --> 00:05:44.601
Trick-or-treat.

00:05:44.601 --> 00:05:45.734
-And you just try to throw a
Reese's through the open window?

00:05:45.734 --> 00:05:47.067
Yeah.
[ Laughs ]

00:05:47.067 --> 00:05:49.701
-Sorry.
Sorry.

00:05:49.701 --> 00:05:51.367
-You're going on tour
with your band?

00:05:51.367 --> 00:05:52.634
-Yeah.
-A European tour.

00:05:52.634 --> 00:05:53.968
-Yeah. Yeah.
-This is very exciting.

00:05:53.968 --> 00:05:55.267
-Yeah. It is.

00:05:55.267 --> 00:05:56.701
-Do you -- have you ever done it
before through Europe?

00:05:56.701 --> 00:05:58.968
-Yeah, we've gone through Europe
and Eastern Europe,

00:05:58.968 --> 00:06:01.467
and this is our second time
touring through Europe.

00:06:01.467 --> 00:06:04.701
And you know, I've tried
to get -- no -- no offense

00:06:04.701 --> 00:06:07.400
to your wonderful drummer
today -- but Fred.

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:09.033
-Yeah. You've tried to
get Fred to drum for you.

00:06:09.033 --> 00:06:11.067
-I can't get any play
out of Fred. Fred is..

00:06:11.067 --> 00:06:12.634
-Really because Fred
says yes to everybody.

00:06:12.634 --> 00:06:15.868
This is shocking.

00:06:15.868 --> 00:06:18.934
He does not -- I mean he is one
of my most talented

00:06:18.934 --> 00:06:20.467
and least discerning friends.

00:06:20.467 --> 00:06:21.667
-Wow.

00:06:21.667 --> 00:06:23.434
I love Fred so much.

00:06:23.434 --> 00:06:27.100
I did a movie with him,
and because he's one person who,

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:29.501
and you can corroborate
this or not,

00:06:29.501 --> 00:06:31.701
you're not sure if he's making
fun of you all the time.

00:06:31.701 --> 00:06:33.000
-That's true.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:34.000
-Yeah.
-But he's not.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:35.334
He's a lovely, sweet guy.

00:06:35.334 --> 00:06:36.334
But you have sinking...
-He's a lovely,

00:06:36.334 --> 00:06:37.501
authentic person.

00:06:37.501 --> 00:06:38.767
-But you never fully trust it.
-...suspicion...

00:06:38.767 --> 00:06:40.501
-Yeah.
-...that he's making fun of you.

00:06:40.501 --> 00:06:41.801
And I don't know
how he does that.

00:06:41.801 --> 00:06:43.400
-The other thing you can be
very confident with Fred

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:45.667
is even if you have the most
wonderful conversation with him,

00:06:45.667 --> 00:06:48.033
he will walk away
having like the best impression

00:06:48.033 --> 00:06:49.567
of you that he's ever had.

00:06:49.567 --> 00:06:51.901
Like he just absorbs a person's
essence and then he can just...

00:06:51.901 --> 00:06:53.234
-I'll have to check that out.

00:06:53.234 --> 00:06:54.501
-Can I ask some questions?

00:06:54.501 --> 00:06:55.667
Because these
are photos of you as --

00:06:55.667 --> 00:06:57.601
You were a front man.

00:06:57.601 --> 00:06:59.801
And these are very --
This is very rock and roll.

00:06:59.801 --> 00:07:03.300
David, I'm very proud of you.
-It's so embarrassing.

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:04.801
-This I don't know.

00:07:04.801 --> 00:07:06.634
This strikes me
as less rock and roll.

00:07:06.634 --> 00:07:09.734
Because that's you.
You're on the bottom here.

00:07:09.734 --> 00:07:12.934
-Yeah, well, you know,
if you get a good guitar,

00:07:12.934 --> 00:07:15.033
if you're lucky enough to get
a good guitarist in your band,

00:07:15.033 --> 00:07:16.734
you've got to treat them well.
-Yeah.

00:07:16.734 --> 00:07:21.400
-And so I carry him around
the entire tour on my shoulders.

00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:22.667
-I'd do it for you,
too, if you're...

00:07:22.667 --> 00:07:24.934
-Oh, yeah.
And you have some drummers.

00:07:24.934 --> 00:07:26.334
You have some,
you mentioned guitarists,

00:07:26.334 --> 00:07:27.968
but there are some drummers
back there who.

00:07:27.968 --> 00:07:30.968
-My band is so big that I have
Ben Stiller on the drums.

00:07:30.968 --> 00:07:32.234
Yeah.

00:07:32.234 --> 00:07:34.434
-So how did you get
Ben Stiller to drum for you?

00:07:34.434 --> 00:07:35.534
I just asked him.

00:07:35.534 --> 00:07:37.834
-And he -- he said yes,
and Fred said no.

00:07:37.834 --> 00:07:40.234
[ Laughter ]

00:07:40.234 --> 00:07:41.367
-Ben was terrific.

00:07:41.367 --> 00:07:42.767
He came and rehearsed
a little bit.

00:07:42.767 --> 00:07:47.467
And then we were -- It was
for the Tribeca Film Festival,

00:07:47.467 --> 00:07:50.133
and I have my drummer.

00:07:50.133 --> 00:07:55.100
And then at some point I said,
"Oh, my God, this is terrible.

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:58.133
My drummer has pulled
a hamstring.

00:07:58.133 --> 00:08:00.300
Is there a drummer
in the house?"

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:02.334
And then people raise their hand
and then like,

00:08:02.334 --> 00:08:04.701
"Oh, this guy, this guy."
And it was -- it was Ben.

00:08:04.701 --> 00:08:06.200
-So there were
other drummers in the house.

00:08:06.200 --> 00:08:07.334
Well, you know,
there's always --

00:08:07.334 --> 00:08:09.267
Is there a drummer
in the house here?

00:08:09.267 --> 00:08:10.801
-Yeah.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:08:10.801 --> 00:08:12.467
[ Laughter ]

00:08:12.467 --> 00:08:16.534
-There is always going to be
a drummer in the house. Yeah.

00:08:16.534 --> 00:08:20.567
-Now, you, you shot
this film in a airport

00:08:20.567 --> 00:08:23.067
and you are also --
You do not care for flying.

00:08:23.067 --> 00:08:24.300
Are you still
a superstitious flier

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:25.334
or have you gotten over it?

00:08:25.334 --> 00:08:27.167
-No, not so much anymore.
But when --

00:08:27.167 --> 00:08:29.767
When I was younger,
before I was acting, I would.

00:08:29.767 --> 00:08:32.567
I would, if I got on a flight
where there was a famous person,

00:08:32.567 --> 00:08:35.434
-Uh-huh.
I would -- I would be...

00:08:35.434 --> 00:08:37.901
I would think, "Oh, this plane
is not going down," because...

00:08:37.901 --> 00:08:38.968
[ Laughter ]

00:08:38.968 --> 00:08:40.100
...because that would be weird.

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:42.968
Like, it doesn't happen
all the time.

00:08:42.968 --> 00:08:44.501
-So you feel more confident

00:08:44.501 --> 00:08:46.100
if there's a celebrity
on your plane.

00:08:46.100 --> 00:08:47.868
-Yeah. Tony Danza, like,
this isn't going down.

00:08:47.868 --> 00:08:48.868
Right?

00:08:48.868 --> 00:08:50.100
[ Laughter ]

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:52.767
-And then when I started
to get famous, I thought,

00:08:52.767 --> 00:08:54.934
"Oh, people are
looking at me like,

00:08:54.934 --> 00:08:56.534
I got to keep this thing up."

00:08:56.534 --> 00:08:59.067
[ Laughter ]

00:08:59.067 --> 00:09:00.501
And I was just imagining, like,

00:09:00.501 --> 00:09:02.133
if the plane started
to have trouble

00:09:02.133 --> 00:09:03.634
and go down,
everybody's like, "It's you.

00:09:03.634 --> 00:09:05.100
You're not famous enough."

00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:07.100
[ Laughter ]

00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:09.767
"Duchovny, you bastard.
You weren't famous enough."

00:09:09.767 --> 00:09:12.133
-You -- You
had a film at Tribeca.

00:09:12.133 --> 00:09:14.567
Was it being a New Yorker
growing up here?

00:09:14.567 --> 00:09:16.467
Is it special to have a movie
premiere out here?

00:09:16.467 --> 00:09:18.234
-It was amazing.
I mean, just walking around

00:09:18.234 --> 00:09:20.934
neighborhoods that I grew up in
and thinking,

00:09:20.934 --> 00:09:23.801
you know, just how life
changes and grows

00:09:23.801 --> 00:09:26.601
and just neighborhoods
that I was a kid in.

00:09:26.601 --> 00:09:28.267
And now I'm doing these things

00:09:28.267 --> 00:09:30.701
and it's just -- it's just
a rich feeling for me.

00:09:30.701 --> 00:09:32.767
-Well, it's very nice.
-Not funny, but rich.

00:09:32.767 --> 00:09:35.133
-Yeah. Not everything
has to be funny.

00:09:35.133 --> 00:09:37.501
David, you're allowed
some richness in your life.

00:09:37.501 --> 00:09:39.133
-I can't.
If I got to keep that plane up,

00:09:39.133 --> 00:09:40.133
I got to be funny.

00:09:40.133 --> 00:09:41.534
[ Laughter ]

00:09:41.534 --> 00:09:44.634
-You still have
your undefeated record.

00:09:44.634 --> 00:09:46.767
So let me check this.
I checked your Wikipedia.

00:09:46.767 --> 00:09:48.801
Not one of your planes
has crashed, is that correct?

00:09:48.801 --> 00:09:50.067
-Yeah. No.
-I'm doing well.

00:09:50.067 --> 00:09:51.834
-Really good.
-Yeah, really well.

00:09:51.834 --> 00:09:53.100
-It's always such
a pleasure to have you here.

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:54.133
Thank you so much, buddy.
-Thank you.

00:09:54.133 --> 00:09:55.501
-Have a great tour.

00:09:55.501 --> 00:09:56.901
David Duchovny, everybody.

00:09:56.901 --> 00:09:58.834
"What Happens Later"
opens in theaters Friday.

00:09:58.834 --> 00:10:00.968
We'll be right back
with Reneé Rapp.

